---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Grad Events & Newsletter Copilot" description="A Brown grad events and newsletter copilot built with Next.js and FastAPI.">
  <a href="/making" class="eyebrow project-back">← making</a>
  <h1>Grad Events &amp; Newsletter Copilot</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    A real-world AI tool for my Brown graduate community work: collect campus events,
    rank what matters for grad students, and turn the result into a newsletter draft I can review.
  </p>

  <div class="project-actions">
    <a class="cta" href="https://sparks-livid.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open tool →</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/keeeeeliu/sparks/tree/main/project-brown-grad-events-copilot" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Source code →</a>
  </div>

  <figure class="project-demo">
    <video
      src="/project_gifs/grad-events-copilot.mp4"
      poster="/project_gifs/grad-events-copilot-poster.jpg"
      autoplay
      muted
      loop
      playsinline
      preload="metadata"
      aria-label="Screen recording of the live Grad Events tool interface"
    ></video>
    <figcaption>End-to-end flow: set dates, fetch events, select social events, and generate newsletter blurbs.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section>
    <h2>Why I Built It</h2>
    <p>
      Each month, preparing the grad newsletter meant scanning scattered calendars, checking for
      duplicate events, deciding what was actually relevant, and rewriting everything into a format
      students could skim. The work was useful, but repetitive enough that good events could get
      missed simply because the search was tiring.
    </p>
    <p>
      This copilot turns that workflow into a focused review pass: fetch the events, structure them,
      dedupe them, score their graduate-student relevance, and leave the final editorial judgment to me.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="project-grid">
    <div>
      <h2>What It Does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Fetches structured Brown calendar events from LiveWhale.</li>
        <li>Deduplicates recurring or repeated listings.</li>
        <li>Uses LLM enrichment to infer audience, category, and grad relevance.</li>
        <li>Ranks events against a target newsletter audience.</li>
        <li>Keeps the workflow human-in-the-loop before anything is published or sent.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Impact</h2>
      <p>
        The first live wave cuts newsletter prep from roughly three hours of manual scanning to
        about fifteen minutes of focused review, while surfacing relevant events that are easy to
        miss during a long calendar pass.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How It Works</h2>
    <p>
      The frontend is a Next.js app deployed on Vercel. It sends date ranges to a FastAPI backend
      on Railway, where the AI pipeline runs server-side so API keys never touch the browser.
      The backend ingests Brown events, deduplicates them, enriches them concurrently with an LLM,
      ranks the results, caches the selected events, and returns a clean JSON response to the UI.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Next.js, Zustand, FastAPI, Python, Pydantic, OpenAI, Vercel, Railway, structured extraction,
      dedupe, ranking, and human-in-the-loop review.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

